{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Do not look behind you.</b> Because Lot and his wife were grieving that they had not also saved all their possessions, therefore it says, ״וַתַּבֵּט אִשְׁתּוֹ מֵאַחֲרָיו וַתְּהִי נְצִיב מֶלַח״ (Genesis 19:26). It should have said 'from behind her' — for her to look. And if according to the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Bereshit Rabbah 50:4) that she became a pillar of salt because she sinned with salt, in that she did not give it to the poor — it is difficult: why was she punished specifically this time? Rather, because now she was grieving over the loss of her possessions and was looking at what would be after her husband's death — this is what is meant by 'from behind him,' for she said that her husband would leave nothing behind him and she would have no place to collect her ketubah from him. Therefore this look became for her a reminder of sin, for the attribute of Judgment prosecuted her, saying: it is not enough that she did not give charity from her possessions — which is likened to salt, after the manner of ״מֶלַח מָמוֹן חָסֵר״ (Ketuvot 66b) — but her eye was also stingy toward the guests, for 'she kneaded cakes' (Genesis 18:6) implies that it was he and not she; and if so it was his wife who sinned, for in the midst of that great upheaval she was grieving over her possessions — not in order to do charity with them, for she was not the kind of person to do so. Therefore she was punished and became a pillar of salt, for in 'salt — money lacking' she sinned, and with salt she was judged. But to Lot he said by way of moral instruction: it is enough for you that you saved your life; and do not be concerned over the loss of the possessions that you leave behind you to the heirs — for why should you look behind you at what will be after you and worry over a world that is not yours, for in the end you will leave your wealth to others. And in this manner we shall also explain, with the help of HaShem, the verse ״לֹא תְפַאֵר אַחֲרֶיךָ״ (Deuteronomy 24:20). And if you still feel pain — in any case, in your lifetime, from where will you eat? From the threshing floor or from the winepress? Concerning this it was said, ״הָהָרָה הִמָּלֵט״ — to Abraham, who dwells on the mountain, for your end will be as your beginning: just as at the beginning, what caused Lot to have flocks and cattle and tents — his dwelling with Abraham caused it — so too now, flee to him and draw near to him, 'then and now.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The citation Bereshit Rabbah 50:4 is given as בר״ר נ ד in the source; resolved to standard parashah-based address. The closing Aramaic phrase וְאִדְהַן ('then and now') retained as Aramaic idiom in the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:01.652563431Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}